
Peace be with you.
Welcome back to this Lenten journey —
a slow, honest walk toward the heart of God.
Wherever you are right now, take a breath…
let your spirit settle…
and let’s step into today together.

Today is Monday of the Fourth Week of Lent —
and today, God speaks a promise
that reaches into the deepest places of your soul:

“I will give you a new heart.”

Not a patched‑up heart.
Not a repaired heart.
Not a heart held together by your own effort.

A new heart.

Today’s reflection says:

“God does not simply repair your heart—He renews it. Lent is a season of inner transformation, where God replaces what is hardened with what is alive, tender, and responsive to His love.”

This is the work God longs to do in you —
not surface change,
not behavior modification,
but deep, inner renewal.

A heart that is alive again.
A heart that can feel again.
A heart that can love again.
A heart that responds to God
with tenderness instead of resistance.

Maybe there are places in your heart
that have grown tired,
or guarded,
or hardened by disappointment.
Maybe there are parts of you
that feel worn down
by the weight of life.

God does not shame those places.
He restores them.

He comes gently,
like a surgeon with steady hands,
removing what is heavy,
softening what has grown rigid,
breathing life into what feels numb.

A new heart is not something you earn.
It is something you receive.

And Lent is the season
where you let God do that work —
slowly,
quietly,
faithfully.

A new heart means new desires.
New strength.
New clarity.
New freedom.
A new way of seeing yourself,
and a new way of seeing God.

This is not about becoming someone else.
It’s about becoming who you were always meant to be —
someone shaped by love,
rooted in grace,
and alive in the Spirit.

Let today be a day of openness.
A day of surrender.
A day where you whisper,
“Lord, renew me from within.”

Because God is not finished with you.
He is forming something new.


So let us pray:

Lord, give me a new heart.
Renew my spirit
and make me whole.
Amen.

Activity for today:  
At some point —
maybe in a quiet hallway,
maybe while you’re washing dishes,
maybe in the car,
or in a moment when no one else is watching —
just whisper:

“Lord, renew my heart.”

Let that whisper become your offering.

Thank you for walking this day of Lent with me.
May God renew your heart
and fill your spirit with new life.
And until we meet again —
peace be with you.
